(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 58-year-old man with diabetes has an asymptomatic brownish patch in the crural region and scaling between the fourth and fifth toes. Under a Wood's lamp the affected skin fluoresces coral-red. What is the causative organism, and what is first-line treatment for localised disease?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus; topical mupirocin","Corynebacterium minutissimum; topical erythromycin","A dermatophyte; topical terbinafine","Candida albicans; topical miconazole","Corynebacterium minutissimum; oral erythromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Erythrasma is a chronic superficial infection of intertriginous skin caused by Corynebacterium minutissimum invading the upper third of the stratum corneum. Coral-red fluorescence under a Wood's lamp is diagnostic, and first-line treatment for localised disease is topical erythromycin or clindamycin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 78-year-old fair-skinned farmer has a small red, conical, hard nodule on the pinna that has ulcerated and grown over two months. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Actinic keratosis","Squamous cell carcinoma","Keratoacanthoma","Amelanotic melanoma","Basal cell carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma is the second most common form of skin cancer, usually following prolonged cumulative sun exposure, and may arise from an actinic keratosis. The classic presentation is a small red conical hard nodule that may ulcerate, or a non-healing ulcer, warty nodule, or irregular pink plaque with haemorrhagic crust. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has red, itchy, circular patches on the forearm, and the clinician is distinguishing tinea corporis from nummular eczema. What is the distinguishing feature?","choices":["Only tinea corporis is pruritic","Only tinea corporis is well demarcated","Nummular eczema clears centrally; tinea does not","Tinea corporis clears centrally; eczema does not","Only nummular eczema affects the limbs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both present as red or pink itchy circular patches. Tinea corporis is a fungal infection with an annular appearance, whereas nummular eczema is non-infectious and coin-shaped without central clearing. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has soft yellow plaques on the medial aspects of both upper eyelids. What do they contain, and what should be done?","choices":["Eccrine duct neoplasms of puberty","Dilated capillaries; laser therapy","Lipid-laden macrophages; no investigation needed","Keratin in an epithelial cyst; excise","Lipid-laden macrophages; screen for hyperlipidaemia"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Xanthelasma consists of soft yellow cholesterol plaques formed by collections of lipid-laden macrophages. They are associated with lipid disorders, so screening for hyperlipidaemia is indicated, and they may signify increased cardiac risk. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman presents on day four of a dermatomal vesicular eruption with new lesions still appearing. Within what window should antiviral therapy ideally be started, and when should it still be given beyond that window?","choices":["Within 72 hours only, never after","Only after polymerase chain reaction confirms it","Only once postherpetic neuralgia develops","Within 7 days, with no exceptions","Within 72 hours, and later if lesions still form"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Antivirals should be started as soon as possible, ideally within 72 hours of rash onset, and treatment is still given after 72 hours when new lesions are forming or there is ophthalmic involvement. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man is found to have onychomycosis of three toenails and a coexisting tinea pedis. He asks whether a cream will clear both. What is the most accurate response?","choices":["Adequate for both skin and nails","Neither site needs treatment","Add a topical corticosteroid to both","Inadequate for both; systemic needed for each","Adequate for the skin, inadequate for the nails"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Topical antifungals are particularly effective against dermatophytes, but topical therapy is inadequate for most scalp or nail infections. The skin disease will respond while the nails require systemic treatment. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with known rosacea reports a gritty foreign-body sensation, blurred vision, and photophobia. What is the most appropriate action?","choices":["Urgent ophthalmology referral","Reassure the patient that ocular symptoms are unrelated to rosacea","Routine ophthalmology referral within three months","Begin oral isotretinoin","Begin topical metronidazole and reassess in six weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ocular rosacea with keratitis, visual symptoms, or corneal involvement warrants urgent ophthalmology referral. Visual symptoms and photophobia raise exactly those concerns. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with diabetes has an extremely painful lesion on the back of the neck with malaise, chills, and a fever of 38.5 degrees Celsius. Examination shows a deep indurated plaque with several separate openings draining pus. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cellulitis","Cutaneous abscess","Carbuncle","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Furuncle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A carbuncle is two or more confluent furuncles arising in several hair follicles with separate heads, and systemic symptoms of malaise, chills, and fever are more common with a carbuncle than a furuncle. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with limited health literacy is being offered several treatment options with different risks. Why must clinicians be able to communicate evidence on prognosis, treatment, testing, and prevention effectively?","choices":["To ensure patients accept the chosen plan","To transfer the decision to the patient","To shorten the consultation","To help patients understand risks and options","To satisfy documentation requirements"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Healthcare providers must be able to effectively communicate evidence on prognosis, treatments, diagnostic testing, and prevention to help patients understand their risks and options. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man has a biopsy-proven invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma on the dorsal forearm. What is the preferred management?","choices":["Topical imiquimod or fluorouracil","Surgical excision or Mohs surgery","Cryotherapy","Observation alone","Radiotherapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery is preferred for invasive squamous cell carcinoma, with at least annual skin and lymph node examination and closer intervals for high-risk or immunosuppressed patients. All suspected invasive lesions are referred to dermatology or an experienced surgeon. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 41-year-old janitorial supervisor reports several weeks of progressively dry, sore hands. She washes her hands frequently and handles detergents and cleaning solutions throughout her shift. Examination shows a glazed, well-demarcated erythematous eruption with scaling over the dorsal hands and distal forearms, ending abruptly at the wrists. She denies any new jewellery, gloves, cosmetics, or topical products, and patch testing performed previously was negative. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dyshidrotic eczema","Irritant contact dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis of the hands","Allergic contact dermatitis","Cutaneous candidiasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Irritant contact dermatitis is the most common form of contact dermatitis and results from chemicals or friction that disrupt the skin barrier. It is frequently occupational, with healthcare and janitorial workers cited as the classic examples, and mild irritants such as soaps produce subacute symptoms progressing over weeks. Hands and forearms are the commonest sites. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 9-month-old boy is brought in for a diaper rash of one week. Examination shows beefy erythema involving the inguinal folds, with peripheral scale and several small papules and pustules on the thighs a short distance beyond the main area of redness. He completed a course of amoxicillin two weeks ago. What finding most strongly indicates the infectious complication rather than simple irritant disease?","choices":["The one-week duration","Fold involvement with satellites","The recent amoxicillin course","Erythema in the diaper area","Scaling at the edge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Whether the skin folds are involved is the finding that distinguishes irritant from candidal disease. Irritant diaper dermatitis affects the convex surfaces and generally spares the inguinal folds because they are less exposed to urine and feces; candidal disease produces beefy erythema in the folds with satellite papules and pustules. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has had six weeks of severe itching followed by numerous tense, firm 2 cm blisters on the trunk, axillae, and inner thighs. Rubbing adjacent normal skin does not cause it to slough. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bullous pemphigoid","Bullous impetigo","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Pemphigus vulgaris","Erythema multiforme"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bullous pemphigoid produces tense 1 to 3 cm bullae on the trunk, flexures, and intertriginous folds after age 60, often preceded by a pruritic prodrome of weeks to months, with a negative Nikolsky sign. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman develops an intensely itchy eruption three days after gardening. Examination shows erythematous papules, vesicles, and a few bullae in linear streaks across both forearms, with lesions at differing stages. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bullous impetigo","Allergic contact dermatitis","Cutaneous larva migrans","Herpes zoster","Irritant contact dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Urushiol sap from Toxicodendron species produces intensely pruritic vesicular lesions in linear distributions where the plant grazed the skin. Lesions erupt within 4 to 96 hours and may appear in multiple stages. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 31-year-old camper develops a prickling sensation lasting about 30 minutes after wading in a freshwater lake, followed 10 to 12 hours later by severe itching and then erythematous papules. What is this condition, and what completes its life cycle?","choices":["Cutaneous larva migrans; from contaminated soil","Tungiasis; a flea penetrating the foot","Cercarial dermatitis; snails complete the cycle","Scabies; a burrowing mite","Bedbug bites from campsite bedding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cercarial dermatitis, also called swimmer's itch or clam digger's itch, results from penetration of the skin by cercarial forms of parasitic flatworms. Host animals such as waterfowl and muskrats pass eggs in feces into water, the eggs hatch and infect snails within 12 hours, and cercariae are released about 5 weeks later. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has a painful vesicular eruption in a band across one side of the chest. What is the single most characteristic distributional feature of herpes zoster?","choices":["It stops abruptly at the midline","It crosses the midline symmetrically","It follows a Christmas tree pattern","It is generalised over trunk and face","It is confined to skin folds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blisters are confined to the cutaneous distribution of one or two adjacent sensory nerves and abruptly stop at the midline. Thoracic dermatomes are most frequently involved at about 55 percent, followed by cranial at about 20 percent. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 6-month-old girl is brought to the clinic by her father for a rash that has been present for three weeks. He reports she is difficult to settle at night and rubs her face against the bedsheet rather than scratching it. Her father has allergic rhinitis and her older brother has asthma. On examination there are weeping inflammatory patches and crusted plaques over both cheeks and the extensor surfaces of the forearms. The antecubital and popliteal fossae are clear. There is no fever, no purulent drainage, and no history of new detergents, soaps, or foods. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atopic dermatitis, infantile pattern","Allergic contact dermatitis","Irritant contact dermatitis","Seborrheic dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis, childhood pattern"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In infants atopic dermatitis produces weeping inflammatory patches and crusted plaques on the cheeks, scalp, and extensor surfaces, and rubbing commonly replaces scratching at this age. The family history of allergic rhinitis and asthma completes the atopic triad, and roughly 80 percent of patients have a personal or family history of atopy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy has three smooth, non-scarring patches of hair loss on the scalp with exclamation point hairs at the margins and fine pitting of several fingernails. A potassium hydroxide preparation of plucked hairs is negative. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Topical minoxidil","Oral terbinafine","Topical corticosteroids","Oral prednisone","Intralesional corticosteroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Topical steroids are first line in children 10 years old and younger. The child is 8, which places him in that group and avoids the discomfort of intralesional injection in a young child. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has an asymptomatic ring of flesh-coloured to pink papules on the dorsum of her hand that has slowly enlarged over months. There is no scale, and a potassium hydroxide preparation is negative. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Sarcoidosis","Nummular eczema","Granuloma annulare","Erythema multiforme"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Localised granuloma annulare produces flesh-coloured to erythematous papules in an annular ring on the dorsal hands, feet, and ankles, and it is characteristically asymptomatic and without scale. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman presents three days after a manicure with rapid onset of redness, swelling, and tenderness of the tissue around her right index fingernail. Purulent fluid has collected under the skin of the nail fold. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["A topical antifungal and dry hands","Oral acyclovir for herpetic whitlow","Warm soaks three times daily alone","Nail plate removal","Incision and drainage with antibiotics"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Acute paronychia starts as cellulitis and progresses to abscess. Severe cases with a purulent collection require incision and drainage, cultures where appropriate to rule out methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, and oral antibiotics such as amoxicillin-clavulanate or cephalexin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man presents with sudden pain and swelling over the sacrum in the gluteal cleft. Examination shows a warm, tender, erythematous fluctuant mass with purulent drainage. What is the appropriate management of this acute presentation?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging before any intervention","Oral antibiotics alone","Incision and drainage","Warm compresses and observation","Immediate wide surgical excision of the natal cleft"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An acute pilonidal abscess requires incision and drainage. The condition arises when a dimple over the coccyx draws in hair and debris leading to follicular plugging, and ingrown hairs prevent drainage and promote abscess formation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has a moveable nodule on the back with a central punctum, which he refers to as a sebaceous cyst. Why is that term a misnomer for an epidermoid cyst?","choices":["It has no lining, being a pseudo-cyst","Enclosed epithelium filled with keratin","It contains lipid-laden macrophages","It is filled with joint mucin","It arises from apocrine glands"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An epidermoid cyst is formed by cystic enclosure of epithelium within the dermis and becomes filled with keratin. It is often called a sebaceous cyst because it appears to contain sebum. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman presents with painful grouped vesicles on an erythematous base. The clinician performs a bedside Tzanck smear, which shows cytologic changes consistent with a herpesvirus. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["A potassium hydroxide preparation","Accept the Tzanck smear as definitive","Confirm with viral polymerase chain reaction","Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin","Bacterial culture and sensitivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A Tzanck smear provides rapid evaluation of vesicular lesions for herpesvirus cytologic changes, but polymerase chain reaction is preferred for confirmation. Viral polymerase chain reaction detects the genetic material of the virus and identifies which herpesvirus is responsible. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman has a blue-grey papule under 1 cm on the dorsum of the hand. What are blue naevi composed of, and how does management differ by lesion size?","choices":["Dermal melanocytes; biopsy larger lesions","Dilated capillaries; blanches","Keratinocytes; cryotherapy","Epidermal melanocytes; always clinical","Lipid-laden macrophages; lipid screening"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blue naevi are composed of deeply pigmented spindle or epithelioid melanocytes in the dermis. Diagnosis is clinical for small lesions and by biopsy for larger ones, with observation and biopsy or excision if changes are noted. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has a firm, bulbous nodule at the site of an ear piercing done two years ago. It extends well beyond the original piercing site and has continued to enlarge, with occasional itching. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hypertrophic scar","Keloid","Dermatofibroma","Foreign-body granuloma","Epidermoid cyst"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A keloid is a fibroproliferative overgrowth of dense fibrous tissue that extends BEYOND the wound margins, develops slowly, continues to enlarge for months to years, and shows no regression. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 45-year-old dishwasher has had swollen, tender nail folds on several fingers for four months, without fluctuance. The nail plates are thickened and discoloured and the cuticles have separated from the nail plate. What is the most common pathogen, and what is the key patient instruction?","choices":["Candida albicans; soak the hands daily","Staphylococcus aureus; keep the hands dry","A dermatophyte; oral terbinafine","Candida albicans; keep the hands dry","Pseudomonas aeruginosa; remove the nail"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic paronychia is an inflammatory reaction of the proximal nail fold to irritants and allergens, and Candida albicans is the most common pathogen. Predisposing occupations include dishwashers, bartenders, cleaners, and cooks, and patients should be instructed to keep the hands as dry as possible. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman is given silicone gel sheeting for a keloid on the chest and asks how it works and how long she must wear it. What is the theoretical basis for using it, and how long is it worn?","choices":["It blocks ultraviolet; worn outdoors","It cools the scar; worn for two weeks","It warms the scar; worn up to a year","It delivers steroid; worn for six weeks","It induces hypoxia; worn for one month"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The theory is that occlusive dressings such as silicone gel sheets increase the temperature of the scar and thereby collagenase activity. They are worn 12 to 24 hours a day for up to a year. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has persistent acne, hirsutism, and irregular menses. What differential diagnosis should be considered?","choices":["Perioral dermatitis","Acne mechanica","Rosacea","Anabolic steroid use","Polycystic ovary syndrome"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Persistent acne in a hirsute woman with irregular or absent menses indicates androgen hypersecretion, and polycystic ovary syndrome is the named differential. Signs of hyperandrogenism should be sought during pre-treatment assessment. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 17-year-old boy starting topical tretinoin and benzoyl peroxide for acne asks how to use them. What education should be given?","choices":["Use only one of the two","Apply together, washing vigorously between","Apply them together for better penetration","Apply both twice daily; clear in a week","Separate them by at least three hours"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Topical tretinoin and benzoyl peroxide should not be applied together because of the risk of skin irritation, and at least three hours should separate them. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man is found on routine examination to have several smooth, firm, deep red papules under 5 mm on the trunk that blanch with pressure. He has never noticed them and they are asymptomatic. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cherry angioma","Melanocytic naevus","Pyogenic granuloma","Nevus araneus","Petechiae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cherry angiomas are acquired vascular lesions formed by capillary proliferation, very common and increasing with age, appearing as smooth firm deep red papules under 5 mm on the trunk that blanch with pressure. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman is given a topical antifungal for a single annular scaly plaque on the trunk. How far should the medication be applied?","choices":["To the visible lesion, stopping when it clears","To the centre of the lesion only","To the lesion and 1 to 2 cm beyond the border","To the whole limb","Distance does not matter with oral therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Topical terbinafine, butenafine, or an azole is applied to the lesion and 1 to 2 cm beyond its border, using the product-specific frequency and duration and continuing through the recommended course after visible improvement. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has a suspected skin allergy, and the clinician must choose between the two allergy tests available in the clinic. What is the correct pairing?","choices":["Both detect delayed type IV reactions","Prick testing for contact dermatitis; patch for immediate","Patch detects fungi; prick detects bacteria","Both are performed on blood samples","Patch testing for contact dermatitis; prick for immediate"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Patch testing diagnoses allergic contact dermatitis, a delayed type IV reaction, while skin-prick testing evaluates immediate, immunoglobulin E mediated allergic reactions. Immunoglobulin E allergy testing evaluates such allergies in the blood. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has several soft yellowish papules with a central dell on the forehead and is worried they are skin cancer. What is the pathophysiology of sebaceous hyperplasia, and what is its malignant potential?","choices":["Capillary proliferation with bleeding risk","Fibrous overgrowth with local invasion","Reduced sebocyte turnover; no malignant potential","Eccrine duct proliferation; low malignant risk","Ultraviolet dysplasia that may become carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sebaceous hyperplasia is a common benign condition in which the turnover of sebocytes, the sebum-producing epithelial cells, changes with age. It has no known potential for malignant transformation, though patients often present with malignancy concern. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man reports three days of burning pain in a band across the left flank, followed by an eruption. Examination shows grouped vesicles on an erythematous base confined to a single band that stops abruptly at the midline. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Herpes zoster","Cellulitis","Bullous impetigo","Herpes simplex virus","Allergic contact dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Herpes zoster is reactivation of latent varicella-zoster virus, preceded by dermatomal dysesthesia or pain, with grouped herpetiform vesicles on an erythematous base confined to one or two adjacent sensory nerves that stop abruptly at the midline. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man is admitted with widespread painful erythema progressing to flaccid bullae and confluent epidermal detachment of about 40 percent of the body surface area, with a positive Nikolsky sign and severe mucosal erosions. What distinguishes this from Stevens-Johnson syndrome?","choices":["Detachment over 30 percent","A prodrome","A drug trigger","A positive Nikolsky sign","Mucosal involvement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The two conditions sit on one spectrum and are separated by the percentage of body surface area involved: Stevens-Johnson syndrome has less than 10 percent detachment, and toxic epidermal necrolysis has more than 30 percent, with mortality up to 30 to 35 percent. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 29-year-old Black man with pseudofolliculitis barbae cannot stop shaving because his employer requires a clean-shaven face. What advice about shaving technique is most appropriate?","choices":["Avoid lift-and-cut razors; use a single blade","Shave daily against the grain","Use a chemical depilatory on shaved skin","Keep shaving; rely on a topical steroid","Use a multi-blade lift-and-cut razor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients should avoid lift-and-cut razor systems and look for mild razor angles with single or at most double blades, because those systems cut the hair below the skin surface where it can re-enter the follicular wall. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman presents with widespread itchy wheals that have been appearing and disappearing for three days. Individual lesions last a few hours, blanch fully, and leave no mark. There is no fever, no joint pain, and no bruising at resolved sites. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Intramuscular epinephrine","Oral prednisone for five days","A first-generation antihistamine","Skin biopsy before treatment","A second-generation antihistamine"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Second-generation antihistamines are first-line for urticaria. Urticaria results from mast cell degranulation releasing histamine, prostaglandins, and leukotrienes, causing transient dermal oedema. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 47-year-old fair-skinned woman reports facial flushing worsened by sun, hot drinks, and alcohol. Examination shows persistent central facial erythema with telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose. There are no comedones. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Perioral dermatitis","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Seborrheic dermatitis","Acne rosacea","Acne vulgaris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The erythematotelangiectatic subtype produces persistent central facial erythema, flushing, and telangiectasias with sensitive stinging skin, triggered by sun exposure, heat, alcohol, and spicy food. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man reports a single 4 cm oval salmon-coloured scaly plaque on his back ten days ago, followed by a shower of smaller similar lesions across the trunk whose long axes run along the rib lines. He feels well. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Nummular eczema","Pityriasis rosea","Secondary syphilis","Guttate psoriasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A herald patch precedes the exanthem in 50 to 90 percent of cases: a 2 to 5 cm oval salmon-coloured plaque that clears centrally, followed 1 to 2 weeks later by smaller lesions in the Christmas tree pattern. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl with varicella is febrile and uncomfortable, and her father asks what he may give her. What analgesic caution applies?","choices":["Give antivirals instead of analgesia","Give systemic corticosteroids","Avoid all analgesics","Avoid acetaminophen; prefer aspirin","Avoid aspirin; caution with ibuprofen"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Management is supportive, avoiding aspirin in children and using caution with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Early oral antivirals are considered for higher-risk patients and intravenous acyclovir for severe disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has head lice that persisted after a first pediculicide application. Why is a multimodal approach recommended, and what does the World Health Organization recommend regarding pediculicide assessment?","choices":["Essential oils are first line","Resistance is falling; read it immediately","Head lice are self-limiting","Resistance is rising; read the result at 24 hours","Oral antibiotics are always needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A multimodal approach is warranted because of increasing resistance, and the World Health Organization recommends that pediculicidal testing be read 24 hours after application. If treatment is not followed appropriately, patients tend to seek alternative care such as essential oils. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 45-year-old fair-skinned woman reports facial flushing that worsens with sun exposure, hot drinks, and alcohol. Examination shows persistent central facial erythema with telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose, and she describes her skin as stinging easily. There are no papules, no pustules, and no comedones. What subtype does she have?","choices":["Ocular rosacea","Acne vulgaris","Phymatous rosacea","Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea","Papulopustular rosacea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The erythematotelangiectatic subtype produces persistent central facial erythema, flushing, and telangiectasias with sensitive stinging skin, and specifically no papules or pustules. It is the most common subtype. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man taking allopurinol for four weeks has fever, stinging eyes, and painful swallowing, followed by painful erythema progressing to flaccid bullae and sheet-like skin loss over about 40 percent of his body surface. Lateral pressure on normal skin causes the epidermis to slide. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Erythema multiforme major","Pemphigus vulgaris","Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Toxic epidermal necrolysis","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Toxic epidermal necrolysis is drug-induced in over 80 percent of cases, with allopurinol the most common trigger worldwide. It produces confluent epidermal detachment over 30 percent of the body surface with a positive Nikolsky sign. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has more than twenty rough scaly papules scattered across a bald, sun-damaged scalp, with no single lesion standing out from the rest. When is field-directed rather than lesion-directed therapy indicated for actinic keratoses?","choices":["When multiple lesions occupy a field of sun-damaged skin","Only after cryotherapy has failed twice","When the lesion is thick, indurated, and bleeding","Only in immunosuppressed patients","When a single lesion has clear borders"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Field-directed therapy is indicated when multiple lesions occupy a field of sun-damaged skin, reflecting that chronic ultraviolet injury produces dysplastic change across a field rather than in isolated spots. Lesion-directed cryotherapy is preferred for isolated or few lesions with clear borders. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who has lost 9 kg without trying is found to have new velvety hyperpigmented thickening of the axillae, neck, and, unusually, the palms and oral mucosa, which developed rapidly over three months. He is not obese and his fasting glucose is normal. What is the most appropriate concern?","choices":["A niacin or corticosteroid effect","Confluent and reticulated papillomatosis","Paraneoplastic acanthosis nigricans","Postinflammatory hyperpigmentation","Insulin-resistant acanthosis nigricans"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Malignant acanthosis nigricans is paraneoplastic, especially with gastrointestinal adenocarcinoma. Rapid onset, unusual sites including the palms and mucosa, and the absence of obesity or insulin resistance in an older patient with weight loss all point away from the common benign form. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has a hard, conical keratotic projection on the dorsum of his hand. What is the key principle in evaluating this lesion?","choices":["Diagnose the lesion at the base","Send the horn; leave the base alone","Cryotherapy without histology","A conical shape means benign","Painless means it can be reassured"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A cutaneous horn arises from the surface of another lesion, which may be benign or malignant — actinic keratosis, warts, and squamous cell carcinoma among them. There is often no clinical feature to distinguish them, so the underlying lesion must be diagnosed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man in Oregon has a painless bite on the forearm that has become indurated with a large surrounding erythematous area. In which region of the United States is the hobo spider the predominant cause of necrotic arachnidism, and what is the early course of its bite?","choices":["Southwest; crampy abdominal pain","Midwest and Southeast; the red, white, and blue sign","Northeast; erythema migrans","Pacific Northwest; a painless bite","Pacific Northwest; immediate severe pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The hobo spider, also called the aggressive house spider, is the predominant cause of necrotic arachnidism in the Pacific Northwest. The bite is painless, with induration and paraesthesia of the bite site within 30 minutes, a large erythematous area, and vesicle formation during the first 36 hours. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man develops widespread wheals within 20 minutes of eating shellfish. What mechanism underlies this reaction, and what class of trigger does it represent?","choices":["Immunoglobulin E mediated, an immunologic trigger","Complement deficiency","Immune complex deposition","A delayed type IV reaction","Immunoglobulin E mediated, a non-immunologic trigger"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Urticaria results from mast cell degranulation releasing histamine, prostaglandins, and leukotrienes, producing transient dermal oedema. Immunologic immunoglobulin E mediated triggers include foods such as shellfish, nuts, and eggs, drugs including penicillin, and insect stings. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with moderate hidradenitis suppurativa of both axillae has not responded to topical therapy. What is the rationale for adding spironolactone?","choices":["A retinoid normalising keratinisation","An antibiotic suppressing the organism","An aldosterone antagonist that lowers androgens","It shrinks sinuses when injected into the lesion","An immunosuppressant blocking tumour necrosis factor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone is an aldosterone antagonist that inhibits ovarian and adrenal production of androgens. Combination birth control pills are used similarly, reducing luteinising hormone and follicle-stimulating hormone secretion. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man reports six weeks of severe itching that is worst at night. Examination shows excoriations and eczematous change of the finger webs, the sides of the fingers, the volar wrists, the axillae, and the scrotum. The head and neck are spared. What is the pathognomonic lesion of this condition?","choices":["Intense nocturnal pruritus","Excoriated eczematous dermatitis of the finger webs","A thin, thread-like linear or J-shaped burrow 1 to 10 mm in length","Linear excoriations on the back, neck, shoulders, and waist","Thick flaking scale containing millions of mites"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pathognomonic lesion of scabies is a thin, thread-like linear or J-shaped burrow 1 to 10 mm long, a tunnel caused by movement of the mite in the stratum corneum. It is best seen in the interdigital webs and wrists, and is hard to see once excoriated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has burning and small bumps around her mouth. Examination shows grouped monomorphic erythematous papules and papulopustules on the chin and nasolabial folds, with a narrow band of normal skin next to the lip margin. There are no comedones. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acne rosacea","Seborrheic dermatitis","Acne vulgaris","Perioral dermatitis","Impetigo"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Perioral dermatitis produces grouped monomorphic papules, papulovesicles, or papulopustules around the mouth, nose, or eyes, with a narrow zone beside the vermilion border characteristically spared. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 22-year-old bartender develops streaky, bizarrely shaped erythema and blistering on the forearms two days after an outdoor shift squeezing limes. The affected areas later become hyperpigmented. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic contact dermatitis to citrus","Polymorphous light eruption","Phytophotodermatitis","Ordinary sunburn","Drug-induced phototoxicity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Phytophotodermatitis follows contact with furanocoumarins, also called psoralens, found in limes, celery, parsley, and related plants, followed by ultraviolet exposure. The streaky shapes trace where the juice ran, and postinflammatory hyperpigmentation follows. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has scaly patches of hair loss, and the clinician suspects a fungal or bacterial infection producing pigment change and reaches for a handheld device emitting long-wave ultraviolet light. What is this device and what does it evaluate?","choices":["A transilluminator, separating solid from fluid","A dermoscope, magnifying under polarised light","A Wood's lamp, evaluating pigment change","A phototherapy unit delivering ultraviolet B","A trichoscope, magnifying the scalp"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A Wood's lamp is a handheld diagnostic device emitting long-wave ultraviolet light to highlight subtle changes in the skin, scalp, and hair, and it evaluates pigment changes in selected fungal or bacterial infections. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man in equatorial Africa presents with rapidly progressive violaceous nodules on both legs. He is not known to have human immunodeficiency virus infection and is not taking immunosuppressive therapy. Which clinical form of Kaposi sarcoma is described as often aggressive and potentially rapidly fatal in young Black men in equatorial Africa?","choices":["The nodular form","The epidemic form","The classic form","The endemic form","The iatrogenic form"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The endemic form is often aggressive disease in young Black men in equatorial Africa and can be rapidly fatal, in contrast to the classic form in older men, which follows a chronic course and is rarely fatal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man treated for pityriasis versicolor returns two months later frustrated that the pale patches persist despite his completing the full course. What is the most accurate explanation?","choices":["Pigment recovery lags months behind clearance","A secondary bacterial infection","It is now contagious to the household","Vitiligo is developing at the sites","It is treatment failure needing oral therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypopigmentation reflects altered melanocyte function and reduced tanning, and recovery can lag months after yeast clearance. The persistence of pallor does not indicate treatment failure. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A clinician seeing a 38-year-old woman with chest pain lists the diagnoses her initial cues suggest, then gathers further data to decide which are consistent with what has been observed. What does the hypothetico-deductive method involve?","choices":["Rank diagnoses purely by frequency","Treat symptoms before a diagnosis is reached","Propose hypotheses, then test them against the data","Apply an if-then guideline rule","Recognise patterns automatically"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The hypothetico-deductive method proposes hypotheses and tests whether they are acceptable or not by deciding whether the data are consistent with what has been observed. It runs from initial history, examination, and screening cues through hypothesis generation to hypothesis evaluation. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man presents with a lesion on the temple that he is unable to describe, and the clinician has not yet formed an impression. What is the recommended first step when evaluating any premalignant or malignant cutaneous lesion?","choices":["Biopsy every lesion first","Name the likely diagnosis, then seek support","Photograph and review next visit","Refer every pigmented lesion","Characterise the lesion before naming it"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The foundational approach is to describe before naming: characterise each lesion systematically by primary lesion type, colour, surface texture, border definition, size, distribution, palpability, ulceration, and bleeding before assigning a diagnosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with untreated human immunodeficiency virus infection has multiple red-purple macules and plaques on the trunk. Oral examination reveals similar lesions on the hard palate. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cherry angiomas","Pyogenic granuloma","Kaposi sarcoma","Purpura","Bacillary angiomatosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Kaposi sarcoma develops in the cells lining blood and lymph vessels and is associated with human herpesvirus 8, producing red or purple macules, plaques, or nodules on skin or mucous membranes. Hard palate lesions are common and may be the presenting site. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 6-year-old has fifteen discrete, smooth, firm, flesh-coloured dome-shaped papules averaging 4 mm on the trunk and arms, each with a central dimple. His parents ask about treatment. What is a reasonable approach?","choices":["Salicylic acid 40 percent to each lesion","Oral acyclovir","Curettage of every lesion at the first visit","Observation; procedures may scar or pigment","Topical mupirocin to each lesion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Molluscum contagiosum is a benign poxvirus infection, and observation is appropriate for many patients because procedures may blister, pigment, or scar. Approved options such as berdazimer gel and cantharidin exist when treatment is chosen. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 136"},{"q":"The mother of a 3-year-old boy with atopic dermatitis asks how to apply the emollient she has been given. What instruction is correct?","choices":["Apply only to inflamed areas","Bathe in hot water first","Withhold on steroid days","Apply after rinsing; avoid fragrance","Use a fragranced moisturiser"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prevention advice is that emollient should be applied to the skin after rinsing, the patient should avoid irritants, and skin care should be fragrance-free. Clinicians should also demonstrate medication quantity and correct application. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has a sharply circumscribed, darkly pigmented papule under 7 mm on the thigh. What is this lesion also called, and what is its typical demographic?","choices":["Spitz nevus, children, face and neck","Blue nevus, adolescence, dorsal hands","Dysplastic naevus, irregular borders","Congenital melanocytic nevus, from birth","Reed nevus, lower extremities in the thirties"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Pigmented spindle cell nevus, also called Reed nevus, occurs commonly in the thirties with females affected more than males, found on the extremities and mainly the lower extremities, especially the thigh. It is a sharply circumscribed darkly pigmented papule usually less than 7 mm. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy presents with widespread superficial skin peeling and a positive Nikolsky sign but entirely normal mucous membranes. What diagnosis does the preserved mucosa most suggest?","choices":["Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms","Erythema multiforme major","Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Toxic epidermal necrolysis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome spares the mucosa and is toxin-mediated, which is exactly how it is separated from Stevens-Johnson syndrome on the differential. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 15-year-old presents with molluscum lesions confined to the genital area. What is the appropriate approach?","choices":["Assume abuse from the location alone","Reassure that it is never sexually transmitted","Refer for excision of all lesions","Assess for other sexually transmitted infections","Give acyclovir and screen only for herpes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Genital lesions in adolescents and adults may be sexually transmitted and assessment for other infections is appropriate. In a child, genital lesions require context-sensitive assessment, and location alone does not prove abuse. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman bitten by a brown recluse spider four days ago has a darkening lesion on the calf. How does a brown recluse bite evolve in the small percentage of cases that progress, and what governs the timing of surgery?","choices":["Full-thickness necrosis within hours; debride now","Resolution without tissue loss","Necrosis at 2 to 3 days, eschar by day 7; delay surgery","Painless induration with vesicles by 36 hours","Systemic symptoms within 30 minutes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In a small percentage of cases the initial wound progresses to necrosis 2 to 3 days after the bite, then eschar formation between days 5 and 7, then deep ulcers. Necrotic wounds heal very slowly and may need surgical intervention or reconstruction, with surgery delayed. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman and her 31-year-old husband both have plaque psoriasis and ask about the likelihood their future children will be affected. What is the most accurate counselling?","choices":["About 41 percent of their children","About 8 percent of their children","No increased risk; it is not heritable","Risk is set by human leukocyte antigen B27","About 50 percent; autosomal dominant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The figures given are 8 percent when one parent has psoriasis and 41 percent when both do, reflecting a genetic predisposition in which PSORS1 is the major susceptibility locus. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 155"}]};
